  "label": "Stress Responsive Organelle Remodeling",
  "related": [
    "age-dependent-huntington-7aeb",
    "mitochondrial-acid-2d6e",
    "specific-topic-8dfd"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "promote-topic-0ce2",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and one JCB report assign specific sensors, tethers and extraction factors to organelle stress responses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that stressed organelles are remodeled through defined protein-level routes rather than a single shared mechanism. In the OXA1L study, copper binding disrupts mitoribosome assembly and releases MRPL20 to the nucleus, where ATF5-driven UPRmt restrains cuproptosis. The ATAD1 study places ATAD1 at mitochondria-ER contact sites in HeLa cells, where it moves mislocalized Pex26 from mitochondria to the ER. A VCP-dependent pathway degrades ER membrane proteins after induced ubiquitination, bypassing canonical ERAD. A dynamic pSILAC knockdown screen of HECT E3 ligases adds a systems view, reporting UPS-wide redundancy, compensation between E3s, and substrates affecting MCL1 turnover. What is new is the assignment of a specific sensor, tether or extraction factor to each context. These items do not establish a shared mechanism across all organelles.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
